Hi there Magda.

{quote}Manually verified (have been using app, verification does not work) Examples: {quote}

What do you mean that verification doesn’t work? I’ve checked both users and they have a high fraud score on Food. Verification is the way to go. They don’t have any payment problems, so I don’t see why verification wouldn’t work. 

{quote}Have been using BF or RH app lately. Examples:{quote}

users 108763794 and 104998981 have a high fraud score. Should either be verified or pass 3ds so the order can be created. You can read more about 3ds here => [https://support.taxify.eu/hc/en-us/articles/360016060340--3DS-process|https://support.taxify.eu/hc/en-us/articles/360016060340--3DS-process]

423643, 17199572 don’t see any challenge or rejection here. It’s all good from the fraud side. What was the user’s complaint?

{quote} Is not able to make an order, does not have any. Examples:{quote}

All three users here have a high fraud score and should be verified or complete 3ds. 

{quote}What can we suggest to those users? (especially to those ones, who were able to make an order lately, besides using another card){quote}

The card is not the issue. You don’t have visibility on your side, but what happened is that we determined that these users were risky. So they have a high fraud score and our action was *Challenge*, which means we’re asking for 3DS. If the user completes 3DS, an order is created. If the user doesn’t pass 3DS or if it’s not available in their bank or anything like that, the order gets rejected. The solution is to verify the users. If the users are not willing to pass verification, we’ll keep challenging. Legit users shouldn’t have any problem with 3DS. 

{quote}Can we verify manually all of them?{quote}

If they pass verification, you should. 

{quote}If after verification user is not still able to make an order, does it mean that eater is fraud?{quote}

No. There could be many different reasons for a user to not be able to order. For example, payment can fail, the fraud score can still be high after verification where we’ll challenge and the user won’t pass 3DS. If the user is already verified and is having issues to order, check the payments log and escalate to Fraud if it’s still unclear. 

{quote}Is there any chance that they will be able to upload an order after some time?{quote}

Sure. The fraud score is dynamic. So if certain parameters change, some rules might not be triggered anymore. But that’s not the solution we want to provide the customers. If they have an issue ordering, start the verification process and verify them if they pass Veriff. 

{quote}If we notice fraud eater, can we blacklist them manually? (we understand that there is a fraud rule that blocks them, but sometimes they still can order){quote}

That happens when they pass 3DS, which changes the liability. So, *NO, do not take any extra action if our rules are already flagging.* There is absolutely no need. If you see a fraud pattern/behavior that is not being flagged by our rules, then feel free to escalate if the pattern is big. 

I’ve checked your example and it was already flagged by our rules and had a high fraud score with a *challenge* fraud action. That action path means that we’ll force 3ds and order will only go through if the user authenticates themselves. If the order has a successful 3ds, if a chargeback is requested, liability will be with the bank and not with us. Basically, despite the suspicious behaviour, these orders have *zero risk* to us. For that same reason, there is no need to block the user, we’re protected.

