 While I can see why agents would follow the lost or found item process (since there was something the user  _found_ in the vehicle), this is actually more to deal with items that a certain user may have  _intentionally_ left in the vehicle. I’ll look into whether we should add a note about this on the Lost and found items process (so agents know to follow the correct process). 

 *As for the bad vehicle condition process*, I already have a Jira task to work on an update to this process (to be released by mid-March at the latest). I will also include what’s discussed on this ticket so we can take care of creating a process for the VW Crafter trash cases, too. 🙂  

Here are my propositions for the process update for handling reports of our VW Crafter vans being left full of trash: 
{color}
* {color:#333333}Currently, CS agents usually escalate the issue to OPS and wait for their instructions - which usually are to find and contact the previous user: 
{color}
** {color:#333333}While the new process update for dirty vehicles includes leaving comments in Admin and contacting the user only at the 3rd occurrence, I believe resolving the trash in our VW Crafters is an urgent issue requiring immediate contact with the user after which we started receiving the issue reports (otherwise, the next users cannot use the trunk to transport their goods) 
{color}
** {color:#333333}When receiving a report with the VW Crafter trunk having lots of garbage of furniture, I think it’s safe to assume that people don’t just  _forget_ stuff like that - most likely, they intentionally leave it there. 
{color}
** {color:#333333}The CS task should be to immediately look for other vehicle reports of the same issue and find out who is the user after which we received the first report - it is likely that this was the user at fault (at least we can safely assume that based on what our T&C say, the user being responsible for the vehicle during their order and reporting anything that’s wrong with the vehicle) 
{color}
* {color:#333333}We should always hear the accused party and actually give them time to clean out the car - that way we don’t come across as accusatory or blunt. We can inform the user of the situation and potential consequences, and help them book the van to go and clean it out to avoid the resulting fine. This 
{color}
* {color:#333333}Only when they refuse to do that, we can assume that the user doesn’t want to take responsibility for the vehicle’s state when they finished the order - and escalate to OPS to issue a fine and have Fleet OPS clean out the car. 
{color}
* {color:#333333}The fine for a dirty vehicle is 69 Eur - even if we end up accusing the wrong user, the fine is in my view low enough to accept losing the user if they react negatively to the fine. Such customers cause unnecessary hassle for Drive, and it can be a very good lesson for the user to not assume that not knowing/checking will free them from the responsibilities. We have a similar approach with users who speed or do other highly suspicious or illegal activities during their orders. 
{color}
* {color:#333333}While we’ve had bad publicity cases (the sofa one, for example, with the user taking the matter to a Facebook group), they were bad because we made our mistakes, too. With that said, if we hear out the potential user at fault, give them time to sort out the issue and inform them what happens if they don’t, then it’s safe to charge them the fine, even if they intend to go public with this. 
